{"name":"Deep Rift","hex":"#4c6a68","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"deep","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Extending, reaching or positioned far from a point of reference, especially downwards.\nExtending far down from the top, or surface, to the bottom, literally or figuratively.","Extending far down from the top, or surface, to the bottom, literally or figuratively."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Far, especially far down through something or into something, physically or figuratively.","In a profound, not superficial, manner."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/deep"},{"term":"rift","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A chasm or fissure.","A lack of cohesion; a state of conflict, incompatibility, or emotional distance."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To form a rift; to split open.","To cleave; to rive; to split."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rift"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rift","blocks":[{"text":"In geology, a rift is a linear zone where the lithosphere is being pulled apart and is an example of extensional tectonics. Typical rift features are a central linear downfaulted depression, called a graben, or more commonly a half-graben with normal faulting and rift-flank uplifts mainly on one side. Where rifts remain above sea level they form a rift valley, which may be filled by water forming a rift lake. The axis of the rift area may contain volcanic rocks, and active volcanism is a part of many, but not all, active rift systems."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Rift_segmentation.png/330px-Rift_segmentation.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rift"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/GulfofSuezRift.png/500px-GulfofSuezRift.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/GulfofSuezRift.png/1280px-GulfofSuezRift.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/GulfofSuezRift.png/500px-GulfofSuezRift.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gulf of Suez Rift showing main extensional faults","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGulfofSuezRift.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/FlankMalawi.png/500px-FlankMalawi.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/FlankMalawi.png/1280px-FlankMalawi.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/FlankMalawi.png/500px-FlankMalawi.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Topographic profile of Lake Malawi","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFlankMalawi.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/20190621_CPH-KEF_7314_%2848453477346%29.jpg/500px-20190621_CPH-KEF_7314_%2848453477346%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/20190621_CPH-KEF_7314_%2848453477346%29.jpg/1280px-20190621_CPH-KEF_7314_%2848453477346%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/20190621_CPH-KEF_7314_%2848453477346%29.jpg/500px-20190621_CPH-KEF_7314_%2848453477346%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Volcano-tectonic landforms connected to rifting on Reykjanes Peninsula, Iceland: faults, fissures, elongated volcanoes of subglacial origin, postglacial lava fields","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2019062